  ],
  "text": "There are millions of fragments in orbit, and a fleck of paint up there hits harder than a bullet. Nothing slows down up there. There is no air to bleed the speed off. So everything ever left behind is still moving at about twenty eight thousand kilometres an hour. At that speed the mass barely matters. A chip of metal punches straight through a pressure hull. And every collision makes the problem worse, not better. Two objects hit, and now you have a thousand objects where you used to have two. Each of those can hit something else. That cascade has a name. Kessler syndrome. Anti satellite tests made this worse on purpose, and left debris that will be up there for decades. The useful orbits are a finite shared resource, and there is no enforcement mechanism at all. We built a rubbish problem in the one place where nothing ever falls. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
